// The REPL end, on a bus. Several of these run at once, each with its own id:
//
//   ./csp -i --id=5 examples/relay/node_bcast.csp
//
// `repl` names the INTERPRETER, not a serial port, so this is what a board with
// no UART would run. Swap the two udp buffers for `can` and it is the same
// three blocks.
//
// Frame layout is master_bcast.csp's, and the reason the header is at the END
// is written out there: a buffer assignment always lands on bytes 0..3.

#buffer Out:4 in  repl                      // what the interpreter printed
#buffer In:4  out repl                      // fed to it, as if typed
#buffer Rx:8  in  udp 55801 127.255.255.255 // the bus
#field  RxArg Rx[0..31]
#field  RxCmd Rx[32..39]
#field  RxLen Rx[40..47]
#buffer Tx:4  out udp 55802 127.0.0.1       // back to the console
#variable Selected = 0

// Every node sees every select. Only the one whose id matches keeps it -- and
// the others must CLEAR theirs, or two nodes answer at once the moment the
// operator switches.
#when Rx.rx && RxCmd == 1
      Selected = RxArg == sys.Id
#end

#when Rx.rx && RxCmd == 0 && Selected
      In = Rx
      In.dlc = RxLen
      In.tx = 1
#end

#when Out.rx && Selected
      Tx.dlc = Out.dlc
      Tx = Out
      Tx.tx = 1
#end
